{{also|armageddon}}
==English==
[[File:Operation_Castle_-_Romeo_001.jpg|thumb|125px|A {{w|nuclear holocaust}} is sometimes associated with modern visualizations of [[w:Armageddon|Armageddon]]]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|grc|Ἁρμαγεδών}}, used in [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_%28World_English%29/Revelation#Chapter_16 Revelation 16:16], referring to [[w:Tel Megiddo|Mount Megiddo]], the place of the last battle at the [[w:Last Judgment|Last Judgment]]; from {{der|en|he|-}} {{he-l|הר מגידו|dwv=הַר מְגִדּוֹ|tr=har megiddo}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɑː(ɹ).məˈɡɛ.dn̩/|/-ˈɡɛ.dən/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɑɹ.məˈɡɛ.dn̩/|/-ˈɡɛ.dən/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛdən|s=4}}
* {{hyphenation|en|Ar|ma|ged|don}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|Christianity|Islam}} {{w|Mount Megiddo}}, the site of a [[prophesied]] final battle between the forces of [[good]] and [[evil]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=October|author=F. S. Bond|title=The Railways of China|journal=Railway Magazine|page=440|text=And so, unfortunately, this great and spreading network of railways, that recently showed such promise as a major instrument in the modern development of China, must be left for the time being in the melting-pot of '''Armageddon'''.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} The battle itself.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any [[end of the world]], especially by way of a [[cataclysmic]] battle or something [[analogous]] to one.

